WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.900
Samsung has been offering wireless earbuds
for a while now, even before the AirPods,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.270
but they never really took off.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:10.249
The battery life was horrible, they had connection
issues, and then the price.

00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:15.019
Well, about a month ago they fixed all of
that and for our 1-month review of the Galaxy

00:00:15.019 --> 00:00:18.461
buds, I’ll have to say that they’re great,
and for many, they’re actually better than

00:00:18.461 --> 00:00:19.461
the Airpods.

00:00:19.461 --> 00:00:22.110
Now don’t get me wrong, the AirPods are
excellent, they’re almost perfect, but there

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.990
are a few ways the Galaxy Buds simply do it
better.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.970
The first and most important thing is the
price.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.770
You’re paying $130 compared to $200 for
the AirPods 2, and that can be a dealbreaker

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:32.820
for a lot of people.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.040
Samsung had to price these competitively,
and they did.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.630
They're mainly marketed to Android users ,but
some iPhone users may even choose to buy these

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.590
over the AirPods 2, so we tested the Galaxy
Buds with an iPhone and made a video on if

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.000
they’re worth it for iPhone users.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.170
Of course, they’re a lot better with an
Android phone because you can download the

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.550
dedicated Galaxy Buds app, which I’ll get
into in just a minute.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.700
Back to the AirPods 2, you can save $40 by
not getting the wireless charging case, but

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.580
the Galaxy Buds have that feature included
for the same price, and the great thing is

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.769
that Samsung’s S10 line of smartphones all
support reverse wireless charging so you can

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.610
charge these without a cable.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.780
Realistically, you won’t always use this,
but it’s nice to know it’s there just

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.920
in case you ever do.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.670
What’s great about the Galaxy Buds is that
they seal in your ears, so while they don’t

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.470
have active noise cancellation, they still
block out a good amount of background noise.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.090
The AirPods feature an open design so you
still hear everything in the background, and

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.870
when you turn the volume up, people around
you can more easily hear what you’re listening

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:26.870
to.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.600
If you’re the Buds with Android, you’ll
have the ambient aware mode and can tweak

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.910
the sound profile.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.100
This is missing on iOS but the default settings
sound best to us.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.549
Samsung also added Apple-like automatic sync
setup, but it's not as simple because of all

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:41.640
the permissions requests you have to accept.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.250
The sealed design of the Galaxy Buds gives
you a lot more privacy, and it also makes

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:49.020
them sound louder than the speakers actually
are since all of the sound is sealed intp

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.480
your ear and can’t escape.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.480
This greatly increases bass reproduction and
it actually helps them save battery life since

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.040
the speakers themselves aren’t as loud,
hence why they’re rated for an extra hour

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.040
of battery life compared to the AirPods.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.090
Because of the seal, they’re also splash
and sweat resistant, just make sure not to

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.823
get any water into the speaker grille on the
inside.

00:02:00.823 --> 00:02:01.823
The AirPods don’t offer this because of
the open design.

00:02:01.823 --> 00:02:04.759
What I really love about the Galaxy Buds is
how subtle they are.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:08.500
They sit flush in your ear with nothing hanging
out or wrapping around your ear.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.600
It’s actually quite impressive how well
they fit and stay in your ears.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.160
And I absolutely love that Samsung decided
not to put their logo on the face of the Buds,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.700
so they look really clean.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.740
Each bud has a touch surface that you can
customize, and overall it works quite well.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.260
You can even change Bixby out for Google assistant.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.040
They come with a charging case that's a bit
larger overall than the AirPods and has less

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.470
battery life, 7 hours of additional juice
instead of around 19.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.980
That is a big difference on paper, but not
as big in the real world unless you use them

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.230
all day long.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:41.810
For most, you can plug them in to charge for
an hour every 3-4 days and you’ll be fine.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.011
For the buds themselves, Samsung rates the
galaxy buds at 6 hours while Apple rates their

00:02:46.011 --> 00:02:47.140
AirPods at 5.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.950
Taking a look at the Sound Guys truly wireless
test, the Galaxy buds are leading the pack

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.870
with 6.5 hours while the AirPods come in at
just over 4 hours.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.319
That's a lot of positives for Galaxy buds,
so what are the downsides?

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:00.769
The biggest one for consumers is the volume.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:04.170
For most quality content they are loud enough
because of the seal and the sound quality

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.100
is great for the price, but I really wish
they were louder.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.730
The AirPods can get loud, too loud actually,
but that comes in handy when the podcast or

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.819
video you’re listening to is too quiet.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:17.959
With the AirPods you have more headroom to
compensate, but in cases like this, the Galaxy

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:19.450
buds can be hard to use.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:25.280
My other complaint is the mic quality, its
usable but doesn’t sound great.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.220
Here’s a comparison.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:34.330
The Airpods mic is closer and pointed at your
mouth, so you get much better mic quality

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:38.150
instead of having the mic flush with your
head which makes it harder to pick up your

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:39.150
speech.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.750
Another limitation compared to the new Airpods
is no hands-free access to your virtual assistant.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:47.230
This isn’t a big deal for me personally
but let me know if it is for you in the description

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.230
below.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.500
Overall, just like with the S10+ and S10e,
I’m really impressed with the Galaxy buds.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.590
They’re not perfect, but considering the
sound quality, convenience, features, and

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.410
price they are an excellent value, even if
you’re an iPhone user.

